Title: BricsCAD macOS automation: open -a app docPath knocks an already-running instance back to the Welcome page
Status: FIX IMPLEMENTED + partially verified live (2026-09-12); full
  end-to-end confirmation blocked by an unrelated, recurring BricsCAD
  hang under sustained automated load — see Round 9 below
Priority: P3
Type: bug
Depends: issues/open/alfe-bricscad-automation-macos-osascript.issue

* What

Found live (2026-09-12) while verifying the Round 8 injection hardening
in alfe-bricscad-automation-macos-osascript.issue. The AppleScript
launcher's LAUNCHCOMMAND (built by %APPLESCRIPT-LAUNCH-COMMAND in
autolisp-front-end/source/backend-bricscad.lisp) does:

  do shell script "open -a " & quoted form of appPath & " " & quoted form of docPath

whenever a template path was discovered — unconditionally, with no check
for whether BricsCAD is already running. When BricsCAD IS already
running with a document open (a realistic, common case: alfe re-run
against the same long-lived instance during iterative testing, or a user
who already had BricsCAD open before invoking alfe), this call can knock
the main window back to its "Début" (Start/Welcome) tab instead of
reusing the existing document or opening docPath as expected — confirmed
by screenshot: the "BricsCAD Lite" window's active tab changed from
"Dessin1" to "Début" immediately after a launcher run, with no new
document tab created for docPath at all.

The console window (Historique des invites) remains visually present
after this, but typing into it then does nothing — no corruption, just
total silence, no error surfaced anywhere. This produced several
seemingly-random live-test failures during Round 8 that were eventually
traced to this, not to the keystroke-injection layer (which was
separately confirmed reliable via hand-written diagnostic scripts that
never called `open -a docPath` a second time against a running
instance).

* Why this matters now

Round 6 (2026-08-14 predecessor decision, referenced in this ticket's
own history) deliberately REMOVED a `set running to exists (processes
whose name is "bricscad")' probe from the template, reasoning that
`open -a` is idempotent for ACTIVATION and no process-name check was
needed. That reasoning holds for activation — but this round found it
does NOT extend to the DOCUMENT-OPENING side effect: `open -a app
docPath` behaves differently (and disruptively) when the app already has
a document open versus a cold start. The two concerns were conflated;
activation should stay probe-free, but opening a document should not be
attempted blindly against a session that may already have one.

* Suggested fix (not yet implemented)

  - Detect whether BricsCAD is already running (a real process check is
    warranted here specifically, unlike the activation case) and, if so,
    emit LAUNCHCOMMAND as `open -a appPath' alone (no docPath) — the
    existing "already open" branch this function already has for when no
    template is discovered at all (see %APPLESCRIPT-LAUNCH-COMMAND's
    `bundle' vs `(and bundle template-path)' branches) — rather than
    conditioning solely on "was a template discovered".
  - Verify live: with BricsCAD already running and a document open,
    confirm the Welcome page is never shown and the existing document
    (or console) stays usable.
  - Also verify the COLD-START case still works: BricsCAD not yet
    running, docPath should still be passed so a fresh launch has a
    document (and therefore a command line) to type into — this is the
    scenario %APPLESCRIPT-LAUNCH-COMMAND's docstring already documents
    as the reason docPath is passed at all ("no document, no command
    line, nowhere for the keystrokes to land").

* Notes / constraints

  - Machine-only, like its parent ticket: needs a live macOS + BricsCAD
    session to verify.
  - Do not re-litigate the keystroke-injection layer here — Round 8 of
    the parent ticket separately hardened and re-verified that machinery
    (window-focus detection, content verification, corrupted-input
    guards, Dock-collision-safe clicking, exception handling throughout);
    this ticket is specifically about the launch/document-opening step
    that runs before any of that.

* References

  - issues/open/alfe-bricscad-automation-macos-osascript.issue (Round 8:
    how this was found, and the six-plus-two injection-layer fixes made
    in the same session)
  - autolisp-front-end/source/backend-bricscad.lisp
    - %APPLESCRIPT-LAUNCH-COMMAND (~line 1150) — builds LAUNCHCOMMAND
    - EMIT-LAUNCHER-APPLESCRIPT — calls it and fills in docPath

* Round 9 — pjb + Claude, live at the machine, 2026-09-12 (same day, continued)

** Fix implemented, refined once after a live edge case, then live-confirmed

First cut: check whether BricsCAD is already RUNNING (`exists (processes
whose name contains "bricscad")`) and skip docPath if so. Emitted
correctly, all existing + one new test green — but the FIRST live test
against a freshly-launched, running-but-no-document-yet BricsCAD (sitting
on its own Start page, exactly the state a cold launch passes through)
showed the bug this coarse check introduces: `alreadyRunning` was true
(the process existed) so docPath got skipped, leaving no document and
therefore no console to type into — the very problem docPath exists to
solve, just reached a different way.

Refined to check something more specific: does a document/console window
ALREADY EXIST — the same exclusion-by-product-name signal
FINDCONSOLEWINDOW already uses (a standard window whose name does not
contain "bricscad", which stays constant and untranslated regardless of
which document is open) — rather than mere process existence. This
correctly covers all three cases: not running at all, running with
nothing open yet, and running with a document open — docPath is passed
in the first two, skipped only in the third.

Verified live: with BricsCAD running and freshly at its Start page (no
document), the fixed launcher's LAUNCHCOMMAND correctly opened
Default-mm.dwt as a new tab (confirmed by screenshot — the tab appeared,
bold/active). This is the core fix working as intended.

** Also found: the console panel can still be CLOSED right after
LAUNCHCOMMAND opens a fresh document, and the existing F2-fallback (from
alfe-bricscad-automation-macos-osascript.issue Round 6) did not reliably
reveal it in that exact sequence, even though the identical F2 keystroke
sent by hand moments later worked immediately. Diagnosed as a timing
issue: \"BricsCAD is the frontmost PROCESS\" says nothing about whether a
just-opened DOCUMENT has finished initializing its UI (ribbon, console
panel) enough to act on F2. Added a `delay 1.5` immediately after the
frontmost-wait succeeds and before the console-finding logic runs — a
pragmatic fixed bound, not genuine polling for document-readiness (a
further refinement, not attempted this round).

** Full end-to-end re-verification blocked by a recurring environmental
hang, not a code issue

After the settle-delay fix, BricsCAD became unresponsive again during
the very next live run — same signature as the hang documented in
alfe-bricscad-automation-macos-osascript.issue Round 8 (process pegged
near 100% CPU, System Events itself still responsive but reports ZERO
windows for the process), but this time after only ~9 minutes of heavy
scripted interaction on a freshly-launched instance, versus hours for
the first occurrence. Two independent occurrences in one day, both
following sustained rapid synthetic clicks/keystrokes/AX queries,
suggests this BricsCAD installation/session genuinely degrades under
that kind of load rather than the first occurrence being a fluke.
Force-quit and left there rather than relaunching a third time this
round — further live confirmation of the settle-delay fix (and a full,
uninterrupted READY->request->DONE cycle through
scripts/verify-bricscad-automation.sh) is the natural next step for a
fresh session, ideally a SHORT one specifically to test whether hang
avoidance correlates with session duration/event volume as
alfe-bricscad-automation-macos-osascript.issue Round 7 already
speculated.

** Net effect

The docPath/Welcome-page bug this ticket is about is FIXED and the core
mechanism verified live (document correctly opened only when needed).
The settle-delay addition is implemented and reasoned but not yet
independently confirmed live due to the hang. Tests: alfe SBCL suite
654/654 green.
